::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
# Endorsements

**Although Maryland school board elections are considered nonpartisan, the Democratic and Republican parties still endorse certain candidates.**

**Jaime Brennan:** Endorsed by Republicans\
**Janie Monier:** Endorsed by Democrats\
**Colt Morningstar Black:** Endorsed by Republicans\
**Josh Bokee:** Endorsesd by Democrats\
**Chad King Wilson:** Endorsed by Democrats\
**Veronica Lowe:** Endorsed by Democrats
:::

## A close race

In the 2024 Frederick County school board election, the Frederick County Democratic Party endorsed four candidates for three open at-large seats. This decision caused splintering within the party that led to conservative candidates -- Jamie Kiersten Brennan and Colt Morningstar Black -- to take two of the seats.

Brennan stepped down as chair of the county chapter of Moms for Liberty to run for school board seat. She won 16.8% of the total votes-- the highest in the county.

Janie Monier was the only candidate with support from the Democratic party to win, racking up 16.7% -- the second highest percentage of votes.

Black ran with conservative support. He won 16.2% of the votes -- the third top candidate.

Josh Bokee lost the race with 16.1% of the total votes. Chad King Wilson Sr. also lost with 14.8%. Veronica Lowe earned the least at 12.5%.

<iframe title="Despite winning more collective votes, only one Democrat-supported candidate won" aria-label="Column Chart" id="datawrapper-chart-CEW2g" src="https://datawrapper.dwcdn.net/CEW2g/2/" scrolling="no" frameborder="0" style="width: 0; min-width: 100% !important; border: none;" height="453" data-external="1"></iframe><script type="text/javascript">(function(){function e(){window.addEventListener(`message`,function(e){if(e.data[`datawrapper-height`]!==void 0){var t=document.querySelectorAll(`iframe`);for(var n in e.data[`datawrapper-height`])for(var r=0,i;i=t[r];r++)if(i.contentWindow===e.source){var a=e.data[`datawrapper-height`][n]+`px`;i.style.height=a}}})}e()})();</script>

## Split precincts

As Frederick County grows in population, the political ideology has started to shift to the left. Vice President Kamala Harris won the county in the 2024 presidential election with almost 53% of votes, compared to President Donald Trump's 44% of votes.

Their performance was closely tied to the geography of the county. Harris performed well in and around the City of Frederick, and Trump performed well in the rural sides of the county.

In rural precincts that voted overwhelmingly for Trump, Black and Brennan performed well.

Democrat-backed candidates performed well in the city, but in precincts where Harris narrowly outperformed Trump, the vote for those candidates split, allowing Brennan and Black to place in the top three.

Harris won 39 of the county's 66 precincts. 

```{r}
#| include: false
knitr::opts_chunk$set(echo = TRUE)

#LIBRARIES
library(tidyverse)
library(janitor)
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)
library(DT)

##SET UP
general1 <- read_csv("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/djnf-data-2026/master/refs/heads/main/data/GENERAL_11PG24_Prov_CVR_SBE_1.csv") |> 
  clean_names()

general2 <- read_csv("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/djnf-data-2026/master/refs/heads/main/data/GENERAL_11PG24_Prov_CVR_SBE.csv") |> 
  clean_names()

general_2024 <- rbind(general1, general2)
school_board <- general_2024 |>
  select(cast_vote_record, precinct, seat_one = board_of_education_500001671, seat_two = x13, seat_three = x14)

seat_one <- school_board |> select(cast_vote_record, precinct, candidate = seat_one)
seat_two <- school_board |> select(cast_vote_record, precinct, candidate = seat_two)
seat_three <- school_board |> select(cast_vote_record, precinct, candidate = seat_three)

school_board <- union(seat_one, seat_two,)
school_board <-union(school_board, seat_three)

## SCHOOL BOARD RACE BY PRECINCT
ranked_school_candidates <- school_board |>
   group_by(precinct, candidate)|>
   arrange(candidate)|>
  summarize(votes=n()) |>
  mutate(precinct= substring(precinct, 2))|>
  filter(
    !is.na(candidate),
    !candidate %in% c("undervote", "overvote", "or write-in:") #needs reporting to verify accuracy
  ) |>
  group_by(precinct) |>
  arrange(desc(votes), .by_group = TRUE) |>
  mutate(rank = row_number()) |>
  ungroup()

ranked_school_candidates_by_precinct <- ranked_school_candidates |>
  pivot_wider(
    names_from = rank,
    values_from = c(candidate, votes),
  names_glue = "{.value}_{rank}")|>
 mutate(
    across(
      starts_with("candidate"),
      ~ trimws(gsub("\\s*\\([^)]*\\)", "",.x))
    ))

percent_ranked_school_candidates_by_precinct <- ranked_school_candidates_by_precinct |>
  group_by(precinct)|>
      mutate(
           total_votes = votes_1 + votes_2 + votes_3 + votes_4 + votes_5 + votes_6,
           percent_votes_1 = round((votes_1/total_votes)*100),
           percent_votes_2 = round((votes_2/total_votes)*100),
           percent_votes_3 = round((votes_3/total_votes)*100),
           percent_votes_4 = round((votes_4/total_votes)*100),
           percent_votes_5 = round((votes_5/total_votes)*100),
           percent_votes_6 = round((votes_6/total_votes)*100)
     )|>
  select(
    precinct, 
    candidate_1, votes_1, percent_votes_1, 
    candidate_2, votes_2, percent_votes_2, 
    candidate_3, votes_3, percent_votes_3, 
    candidate_4, votes_4, percent_votes_4, 
    candidate_5, votes_5, percent_votes_5, 
    candidate_6, votes_6, percent_votes_6)


#PRESIDENTIAL RACE ANALYSIS BY PRECINCT

general_president_clean <- general_2024|>
  select(precinct, president=president_and_vice_president_of_the_united_states_500001630)|>
  group_by(precinct, president)|>
     arrange(president)|>
  summarize(votes=n()) |>
  mutate(precinct= substring(precinct, 2))|>
  filter(
    !is.na(president),
    !president %in% c("undervote", "overvote", "or write-in:") #needs reporting to confirm accuracy
  ) |>
  group_by(precinct) |>
  arrange(desc(votes), .by_group = TRUE) |>
  mutate(rank = row_number()) |>
  ungroup()|>
  mutate(
    across(
            starts_with("president"),
               ~ trimws(gsub("\\s*\\([^)]*\\)", "",.x))))|>
  pivot_wider(
    names_from = rank,
    values_from = c(president, votes),
  names_glue = "{.value}_{rank}")|>
      mutate(
           total_votes = votes_1 + votes_2 + votes_3 + votes_4 + votes_5,
           percent_votes_1 = round((votes_1/total_votes)*100),
           percent_votes_2 = round((votes_2/total_votes)*100),
           percent_votes_3 = round((votes_3/total_votes)*100),
           percent_votes_4 = round((votes_4/total_votes)*100),
           percent_votes_5 = round((votes_5/total_votes)*100)
     )
  
#how many precincts did harris win? 39
harris_win <- general_president_clean |>
  filter(president_1 == "Harris-Walz")|>
  select(precinct, president=president_1, harris_votes=votes_1, harris_percent=percent_votes_1)

#harris precincts + their school board results 
harris_w_board <- harris_win |>
  left_join( percent_ranked_school_candidates_by_precinct, by = "precinct")|>
  select(precinct, president, 
         candidate_1, 
         candidate_2,
         candidate_3)
         
#how many did she win that  voted for one of the conservative candidates? 15
harris_w_mol<- harris_w_board|> 
  filter(
    candidate_1 %in% c("Jaime Kiersten Brennan", "Colt Morningstar Black") | candidate_2 %in% c("Jaime Kiersten Brennan", "Colt Morningstar Black") | candidate_3 %in% c("Jaime Kiersten Brennan", "Colt Morningstar Black")
  )



```

### Some 15 precincts supported Harris and conservative-supported school board candidates

```{r}
#| echo: false
harris_w_mol|>
  datatable()|>
  formatStyle(
    columns = c("candidate_1", "candidate_2", "candidate_3"),
    backgroundColor = styleEqual(c("Jaime Kiersten Brennan", "Colt Morningstar Black"), c('red', 'red'))
  )

```

## Relevance

This demonstrates that, despite Frederick’s growth in recent years that has skewed its politics to the left, the conservative values in the county still remain strong when it comes to local government.

Identifying where the split among voters happened will better contextualize the upcoming school board election for readers, parents and candidates. It will be important to keep an eye on what the Democrats do differently this election season as a result.

## Previous coverage

From what I’ve seen, there has not been much after-the-fact coverage on this race in particular, other than generalized statewide coverage about the Democrats’ failures in school boards throughout the state from Maryland Matters. There is some other pre-election coverage from Maryland Matters and a feature on the conservative candidates' ideologies from the Capital News Service as well.


[Md. Democratic Party targets 'extreme candidates ' in local school board races - Maryland Matters](https://marylandmatters.org/2024/10/17/md-democratic-party-targets-extreme-candidates-in-local-school-board-races/)

[Support for book bans varies in Maryland school board races](https://cnsmaryland.org/2024/10/21/support-for-book-bans-varies-in-maryland-school-board-races/)

 [Half of ‘extreme’ school board candidates targeted by Md. Democratic Party win their races - Maryland Matters](https://marylandmatters.org/2024/11/08/half-of-extreme-school-board-candidates-targeted-by-md-democratic-party-win-races/).


## To learn

I need to do more research on the previous board members, why those seats were open and what the 2024 campaign season was like. I’d also like to research what kind of decisions or changes the new conservative school board members have made since being elected to office and what new candidates are saying about them. I've seen that Black made a controversial comment in 2025, and Frederick County Public Schools released a statement to [disavow](https://www.fcps.org/about/news/board_of_education_statement) what he said.

I want to learn more about what voters are concerned about now compared to then, and I also want to see what candidates this election cycle are saying. Chad King Wilson is running again. Wilson lost in 2024 but was appointed by the County Council to fill an open seat in January. He is running to keep his spot.

